1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,100
Japanese has many sound words, such as kira-kira for sparkling.

2
00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:12,500
They are useful and fun, but they can be hard to remember from a list.

3
00:00:15,400 --> 00:00:21,500
SingLink turns these words into a short quiz and karaoke experience.

4
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:30,100
First, you choose one of five familiar melodies. Then you answer four Japanese questions.

5
00:00:30,900 --> 00:00:36,000
After the quiz, Zundamon sings the four correct sentences.

6
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:42,300
The Japanese lyrics light up with the melody, and English subtitles explain the meaning.

7
00:00:42,600 --> 00:00:50,600
[SingLink karaoke sample]

8
00:00:51,400 --> 00:01:00,200
During OpenAI Build Week, I rebuilt important parts for the Web.

9
00:01:00,200 --> 00:01:08,500
I prepared 335 learning records with original examples, meanings, singing readings, and English support.

10
00:01:08,500 --> 00:01:14,600
I also added a responsive English interface, five melody choices, and score-note timing.

11
00:01:15,400 --> 00:01:22,500
Judges can open a fixed demo on GitHub Pages with no setup.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:28,100
It uses four fixed questions and one pre-generated singing sample.

13
00:01:28,100 --> 00:01:35,500
Normal mode chooses four random questions and creates a new song locally from their correct sentences with VOICEVOX.

14
00:01:36,400 --> 00:01:47,800
I used Codex and GPT-5.6 to study the old app, move the score logic to the Web, check the data, build the English features, and test the result.

15
00:01:47,800 --> 00:01:54,500
I made the final learning, design, and publishing decisions.

16
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:03,000
SingLink connects Japanese words, sound, and meaning through karaoke.

17
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:08,000
It gives learners one more way to repeat and remember.

18
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:12,200
You can try the public demo from the link on screen.
